WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.690
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:08.970
I am really excited to show this to you as
I was looking to make this episode for quite

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:09.970
a while.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:13.140
You'll see lots of beautiful slow-motion footage
during the narration.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:18.520
And at first, it may seem disconnected from
the narrative, but by the end of video, you'll

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.980
understand why they look the way they do.

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:28.000
Now, before we proceed, let's talk about the
difference between interpolation and extrapolation.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.360
Interpolation means that we have measurement
points for a given quantity, and we'd like

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.969
to know what happened between these points.

00:00:34.969 --> 00:00:40.190
For instance, we have two samples of a person's
location at four and at five o'clock, and

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.129
we'd like to know where the guy was at four
thirty.

00:00:43.129 --> 00:00:48.680
However, if we're doing extrapolation, we're
interested in guessing a quantity beyond the

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:50.959
reach of our sample points.

00:00:50.959 --> 00:00:56.149
For instance, extrapolation would be predicting
what happens after the very last frame of

00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:57.149
the video.

00:00:57.149 --> 00:01:01.559
In our earlier episode, we talked about financial
extrapolation, make sure to have a look, it

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:02.739
was super fun.

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:04.920
The link is in the video description.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.090
Optical flow is really useful because it can
do this kind of interpolation and extrapolation

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.320
for images.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.909
So let's do one of them right now.

00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:16.140
You'll see which it will be in a second.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:21.310
So this is a classical scenario that we often
encounter when producing a new Two Minute

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:27.820
Papers episode - here, we have a 25 or 30
frames per second video on a 60 frames per

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.340
second timeline.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.360
This means that roughly only every other frame
is duplicated, and offers no new information.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.020
You can see this as I step through these individual
frames.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:43.510
The more astute Fellow Scholars immediately
would point out that wait, we have a lot of

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:48.509
before and after image pairs, so we could
do a lot better!

00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:52.229
Why don't we try to estimate what happened
between these images?

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:56.420
And that is exactly what we call frame interpolation.

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.990
Interpolation because it is something between
two known measurement points.

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:04.569
And if we run the optical flow algorithm that
can accomplish this, we can fill in these

00:02:04.569 --> 00:02:17.150
doubled frames with new ones that actually
carry new information.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:20.239
So the ratio here was roughly two to one.

00:02:20.239 --> 00:02:23.670
Roughly every other frame provides new information.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.510
Super cool!

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:31.459
So, what are the limits of this technique?

00:02:31.459 --> 00:02:36.980
What if we artificially slow the video down,
so that it's much longer, so not only every

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.970
other, but most of the frames are just duplicates?

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:44.110
This results in a boring and choppy animation.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:45.830
Can we fill those in too?

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:50.970
Note that the basic optical flow equations
are written for tiny changes in position,

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:56.450
so we shouldn't expect it to be able to extrapolate
or interpolate any quantity over a longer

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:57.849
period of time.

00:02:57.849 --> 00:03:01.939
But of course, it always depends on the type
of motion we have at hand, so let's give it

00:03:01.939 --> 00:03:18.620
a try!

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:23.760
As you can see, with optical flow, the algorithm
has an understanding of the motions that take

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.939
place in the footage, and because of that,
we can get some smooth, buttery, slow motion

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:31.860
footage that is absolutely mesmerizing.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.450
Almost like shooting with a slow motion camera.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:39.301
And note that the majority of these frames
were not containing any information, and this

00:03:39.301 --> 00:03:44.050
motion was synthesized from these distant
sample points that are miles and miles away

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:45.760
from each other.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.690
However, it is also important to point out
that optical flow is not a silver bullet and

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:57.340
it should be used with moderation and special
care as it can also introduce nasty artifacts

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:58.859
like the one that you see here.

00:03:58.859 --> 00:04:03.970
This is due to an abrupt, high frequency change
that is more difficult to predict than a slow

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:06.860
and steady translation or rotation motion.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:12.189
To avoid these cases, we can use a much simpler
frame interpolation technique that we call

00:04:12.189 --> 00:04:13.410
frame blending.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:17.780
This is a more naive technique that doesn't
do any meaningful guesswork and computes the

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:19.900
average of the two results.

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:21.780
Why don't we give this one a try too?

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.570
Or even better, let's have a look at the difference
between the original choppy footage, and the

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:30.260
interpolated versions with frame blending
and optical flow.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:35.241
If we do that, we see that frame blending
is unlikely to give us nasty artifacts, but

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:40.520
in return, the results are significantly more
limited compared to optical flow because it

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.130
doesn't have an understanding of the motion
taking place in the footage.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.830
So, the question is, when to use which?

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:52.201
Well, until we get an algorithm that is able
to adaptively decide when to use what, it

00:04:52.201 --> 00:04:57.580
still comes down to individual judgement and
sometimes quite a bit of trial and error.

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:01.540
I'd like to make it extremely sure that you
don't leave this video thinking that this

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:04.220
is the only application of optical flows.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:06.010
It's just one of the coolest ones!

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:09.820
But this motion estimation technique also
has many other uses.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:14.750
For instance, if we have an unmanned aerial
vehicle, it's really great if we can endow

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:19.780
it with an optical flow sensor, because then,
it will be able to know in which direction

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:25.130
it needs to rotate to avoid a tree, or whether
it is stable or not at a given point in time.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:30.200
And, with your support on Patreon, we were
not only able to bump up the resolution of

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:35.830
future Two Minute Papers episodes to 4K, but
we're also running them at true 60 frames

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:41.380
per second, which means that every footage
can undergo either a frame blending or optical

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:45.700
flow step to make the animations smoother
and more enjoyable for you.

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:51.170
This takes a bit of human labor and is computationally
expensive, but our new Two Minute Papers rig

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:52.990
is now capable of handling this.

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:58.580
It is fantastic to see that you Fellow Scholars
are willing to support the series, and through

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:02.990
this, we can introduce highly desirable improvements
to the production pipeline.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:06.960
This is why we thank you at the end of every
episode for your generous support.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:10.400
You Fellow Scholars are the best YouTube audience
anywhere.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.880
And who knows, maybe one day, we'll be at
a point where Two Minute Papers can be a full

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.130
time endeavor, and we'll be able to make even
more elaborate episodes.

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:24.230
As I am tremendously enjoying making these
videos, that would be absolutely amazing.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:28.860
Have you found any of these disturbing optical
flow artifacts during this episode?

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:32.160
Have you spotted some of these in other videos
on YouTube?

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.380
Let us know in the comments section so we
can learn from each other.

00:06:35.380 --> 00:06:39.220
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

